			"allod/gipat": {
				"kind": "allod",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Gipath is an unclaimed allod of scattered ruins, mountains and wilderness. Its small human village shares the island with goblins, orcs and lizardmen. The apparent remoteness hides older history: Joon remains, the Dead City and the dwelling of a Great Magician all lie on the same island.",
						"sources": [
							"allod gipat",
							"zone gz9g",
							"briefing z11_1"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Awakening among the ruins",
						"text": "[[character/hero]] begins his journey on [[allod/gipat]], without knowing who he is or how he arrived. His first concern is finding other people. Leaving the ruins, he sees villagers flee from the stranger who has emerged from the ancient site. Their flight gives him a direction to follow. He makes his way along the river and across the ford to the village.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q0g",
							"briefing s1",
							"briefing s0"
						],
						"event": "awakening",
						"order": 1
					},
					{
						"heading": "The village and the Chosen",
						"text": "At the village assembly, [[character/elder]] identifies Zak with the promised Chosen who was to appear at the Sacred Ruins. [[character/sheriff]], the captain, supports the ceremony. The offer of food, shelter and honour comes with a threat: an impostor would be thrown into the fire. Zak accepts the title and gives his name. Erfar immediately sends him against the goblin sentry watching the village, reasoning that his plain clothes and unusual metal knife will let him approach where armed villagers cannot. Afterwards Zak learns that this is Gipath’s only human village, with no king or known route to other lands.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s1",
							"briefing s0",
							"briefing v0",
							"quest q1ag"
						],
						"event": "chosen",
						"order": 2
					},
					{
						"heading": "The wolf, the captain and the craftsman",
						"text": "Killing the sentry brings another request from Erfar: the White Wolf and its pack are taking the village livestock. After Zak kills their leader, Erfar and Gort press him to surrender the head so the captain can claim prestige. Gort also sends him to punish the goblins while the village soldiers stay home. Meanwhile [[character/smith]] refuses to work until his stolen iron tools are returned. Erfar calls the thieves friendly “Free Hunters”; Babur calls them brigands and describes the beating of his apprentice. Recovering the chisel and bodkin from the chief and his wife wins Zak the craftsman’s services. These early errands expose the disagreement between the village’s leaders and the man who equips it.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s1_3",
							"briefing s3",
							"briefing v3",
							"briefing v3_1",
							"briefing m1",
							"briefing m1_1",
							"briefing m4",
							"quest q2g"
						],
						"event": "village_errands",
						"order": 3
					},
					{
						"heading": "The brigands’ trail and the Magic Bit",
						"text": "Babur’s account of a red-haired brigand meeting orcs gives Zak his next lead. A map taken from Redhead points to a Magic Bit in a sarcophagus in the orc cave. Babur explains that Estera values such objects, while Gort can supply the missing drawbridge mechanism. Zak obtains the seer’s key and the object, then asks how to reach the witch. Babur, who made her spare trap key, explains the guarded ravines and controlling obelisks. The craftsman expects a share of the sale. Zak instead intends to exchange the find for help with his lost memory.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing m4",
							"briefing m5_1",
							"briefing v4",
							"briefing m6",
							"briefing m6_1",
							"quest q4g",
							"quest q5g",
							"quest q6g"
						],
						"event": "magic_bit",
						"order": 4
					},
					{
						"heading": "The seer and the pyramid",
						"text": "Babur also offers a separate reward for weakening the orcs in the Foothills. Their seer controls an ancient pyramid that strikes intruders with lightning. If Zak accepts, he kills the seer and shuts the device down, allowing the craftsman to travel more safely. When Babur pays him, Zak asks why a land without a king uses royal coins. Babur explains that the money survives from an earlier civilisation. Accessible ruins have already been stripped; richer finds lie in places dangerous enough to discourage even him.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing m5_3",
							"briefing m5_4",
							"quest q5dg"
						],
						"event": "seer_and_pyramid",
						"order": 5
					},
					{
						"heading": "Meeting Estera and Airy",
						"text": "Before Estera’s formal introduction, Zak can meet Airy at the cave; she warns him about her grandmother and the traps protecting their home. [[character/witch]] initially threatens the intruder, then changes her reception when Zak offers the Magic Bit. She dismisses the villagers’ divine-saviour story but discovers that she cannot see his past. Since Zak cannot remember it either, she suggests finding the orcs’ Magic Orb and directs him back to Erfar for information. This is the beginning of their alliance. Estera also introduces her granddaughter [[character/daughter]], warning Zak to keep his distance; Airy resents being treated as a child. Zak’s search now has a personal aim beyond the village’s demands.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z1",
							"briefing z1_2",
							"briefing z0_2",
							"briefing m6_1",
							"briefing z0",
							"briefing z0_1"
						],
						"event": "estera",
						"order": 6
					},
					{
						"heading": "Two searches on the islands",
						"text": "The river islands hold two different leads. Estera needs the Orb, which Erfar places on the southernmost island. Erfar also proposes tracing [[character/bgoblinc]]’s blue Eye Stone to a possible portal. Zak takes the First Goblin’s skull to compel GoGo to visit the village; the chief explains that the orcs confiscated the written record of the stone’s origin. The islands require their own bridge mechanism, distinct from the cave’s. Zak retrieves the record and the Orb in this area. Erfar claims the record offers nothing useful, then diverts attention to missing taxes and gold in Golden Valley. His portal scheme and Estera’s search serve different purposes, even though they take Zak through the same territory.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s6_2",
							"briefing s7_1",
							"briefing s7_4",
							"briefing s8_2",
							"briefing v5",
							"quest q7g",
							"quest q8g",
							"quest q9g",
							"quest q10g"
						],
						"event": "orb_islands",
						"order": 8
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Orb and the Dead City",
						"text": "When Estera uses the Orb, Zak sees an old man instead of a recognisable memory. She believes he is its owner and a more powerful magician who might help. Orc records trace the object to the Dead City. Estera distinguishes its human fortress from Gipath’s much older ruins: after the Night of Troubles, the dead attacked the living, and survivors fled to found the present village. The obvious route into the fortress is deadly. Airy leaves to investigate another way in, despite her grandmother’s objections. Separately, Erfar can send Zak into the surrounding region’s Golden Valley for a caravan’s lost treasure, an errand he badly understates.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z2",
							"briefing z11",
							"briefing z11_1",
							"briefing s8_2",
							"briefing s12",
							"briefing s12_1",
							"quest q12g"
						],
						"event": "dead_city_lead",
						"order": 10
					},
					{
						"heading": "Golden Valley and the missing apprentice",
						"text": "Erfar links the missing village taxes to the disappearance of Babur’s apprentice and accuses the craftsman of plotting to take power. Babur denies the charge: he suspects the brigands of stealing the gold and kidnapping his worker. Erfar proposes replacing the money with treasure from a ruined caravan in Golden Valley. Babur exposes the danger omitted from this offer: earlier visitors vanished, and one survivor returned deranged, speaking of undead guards. Zak can refuse the expedition. Erfar then reproaches him and later claims another man supplied the gold. If Zak instead retrieves it, the elder thanks him with promises of a song and admits that he understated the danger. Neither exchange establishes what actually happened to the apprentice.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s8_2",
							"briefing m12",
							"briefing m12_1",
							"briefing s12",
							"briefing s12_1",
							"briefing s12_3",
							"quest q12g"
						],
						"event": "golden_valley",
						"order": 11
					},
					{
						"heading": "The supposed attack on the goblins",
						"text": "While Zak seeks the magician, Erfar and Gort arrange another joint operation with the Free Hunters, supposedly to prevent a goblin raid. The promised friendly meeting becomes an ambush by brigands and village soldiers. Erfar denies responsibility. Gort blames drunken, undisciplined soldiers and brigands dressed as villagers, then briefly threatens to overthrow Erfar before withdrawing the threat. Babur warns that the elder is trying to have Zak killed. A note in the newer orc script cannot yet settle the matter: Estera needs an Orc ABC Book to read it. Its translation reveals a contract on Zak’s life, a detailed description of him and a substantial payment. The search for the author becomes a separate investigation alongside the route into the Dead City.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s12_4",
							"briefing s13_0",
							"briefing m13_3",
							"briefing z13_2",
							"briefing z14",
							"quest q13ag",
							"quest q14g",
							"briefing v13_0",
							"briefing v13_1",
							"briefing v13_2"
						],
						"event": "ambush",
						"order": 12
					},
					{
						"heading": "Airy’s route through the observatory",
						"text": "Airy returns with a lead to the old observatory library, but harpies occupy the site. Her plan requires a lizard’s amulet to lead a particular green dragon against them. Reaching that dragon requires Estera to freeze the lake with a green crystal. The hostile lizards on the approach present another obstacle: Zak moves the Big Shell from the Remote Mountains to the Middle Mountains, restoring the peace associated with its altar. This leaves Babur’s former quarry route unsafe and can lead to an additional errand for the craftsman. The observatory book finally supplies the password to the Dead City’s hidden entrance. Airy can also teach Zak the Magic of Feelings, only to be offended when he again calls her a little girl.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z13_1",
							"briefing z14_1",
							"briefing m13_2",
							"briefing m15",
							"briefing z17",
							"briefing z15",
							"briefing z18",
							"quest q15ag",
							"quest q15bg",
							"quest q18g"
						],
						"event": "observatory",
						"order": 13
					},
					{
						"heading": "Reopening Babur’s quarry",
						"text": "Moving the Big Shell solves one problem and creates another. Babur can no longer use the road protected by its old altar. The alternative route passes a cyclops tending pigs stolen from the village, and the craftsman offers payment to make it safe. Zak can kill the cyclops or destroy its food supply and wait for it to leave. The latter plan depends on remaining unnoticed. Babur pays once access is restored. The dispute shows why his advice about the Shell serves his own livelihood as well as Zak’s journey.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing m15",
							"briefing m16",
							"quest q16g"
						],
						"event": "quarry",
						"order": 14
					},
					{
						"heading": "Gort’s rebellion",
						"text": "The written instructions to kill Zak expose Gort’s handwriting. Confronted with the evidence, the captain turns against the authority he formerly served and declares that he will rule the village himself. He flees to gather the forest men. Erfar urges Zak to stop him before he can return; the ensuing pursuit ends Gort’s rebellion. [[character/nsheriff]] becomes the new captain. Unlike his predecessor, Rivar admits that he dislikes fighting, shares his family’s secret of strength and later supports Zak when suspicion finally reaches the elder.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing v19",
							"briefing v19_1",
							"briefing m21",
							"briefing s21",
							"briefing nv23",
							"briefing nv26",
							"quest q19g",
							"quest q23g"
						],
						"event": "gort",
						"order": 15
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Old Dragon on Lone Mountain",
						"text": "The observatory’s password takes Zak into the Dead City and up Lone Mountain. He expects the magician from the Orb but finds [[character/skd]]. The dragon insists on an old bargain: a visitor must perform a task before receiving an answer. Zak deals with the armadillos beneath the lair, using the poison the dragon supplies. The dragon then admits that he cannot identify Zak or locate Gipath’s elusive Great Magician. He suggests the ancient lizards might know more and points instead to an expedition of armoured outsiders. He withdraws the skeletons he had used against their camp. In a further conversation, he gives Zak an old sword because he enjoys his company and would rather not see him become another speechless skeleton.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q20g",
							"briefing dr20",
							"quest q22g",
							"briefing dr22",
							"briefing dr22_1"
						],
						"event": "dragon",
						"order": 16
					},
					{
						"heading": "The White Flower and Erfar’s exposure",
						"text": "After Gort’s fall, Erfar claims that an orc seer has cursed the village with disease and asks for a White Flower. Babur wants to monopolise the healing flower and charge Erfar ruinous prices; Zak rebukes him. Zak finds armed opposition waiting for him, although no villagers have yet fallen ill. Erfar blames Babur, while Babur’s earlier warning makes Zak suspect both men. Estera suggests setting a trap. Zak tells each suspect a different location for a second flower; the ambush appears at the place he gave Erfar. With Rivar’s support he confronts and imprisons the elder. An expedition report recovered from the abandoned foreign camp becomes leverage: Erfar translates its account of a concealed tunnel and its opening lever. He later escapes custody. Estera recalls that he originally arrived as an adult stranger, leaving his real purpose unresolved. Babur welcomes the exposure and says that the villagers feared Erfar rather than loved him.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s21_1",
							"briefing s24",
							"briefing m24",
							"briefing z24_1",
							"quest q25g",
							"briefing nv26",
							"briefing s25_1",
							"briefing s25_3",
							"briefing nv27_1",
							"briefing z25",
							"quest q26g",
							"briefing m21_1",
							"briefing m26"
						],
						"event": "erfar_exposed",
						"order": 17
					},
					{
						"heading": "The expedition and the Hermit’s bargain",
						"text": "Estera introduces a lost Khadaganian [[character/clerk|official]], who promises that his expedition can help Zak leave Gipath. Meeting [[character/hwleader]] instead leads to imprisonment and demands for information about a meteorite. Zak plays the ignorant swineherd, but the commander threatens torture. [[character/oliz]] offers escape on the condition that Zak later visit his home. Zak gives his word, receives the Ancient Claw for the traps on the approach and escapes. The Hermit explains that he had intended to negotiate with the armoured humans but changed his mind after seeing their treatment of Zak. Keeping the promise establishes trust where the expedition failed to do so.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z27",
							"briefing ha29",
							"briefing ha29_1",
							"briefing ha29_2",
							"briefing ha29_3",
							"briefing l30"
						],
						"event": "expedition",
						"order": 18
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Hermit’s gift and dangerous joke",
						"text": "The Hermit offers more than directions. He gives Zak an object resembling a horn or shell, hoping it will help him survive, although he cannot explain how a human should use it. His advice about treasure is less benevolent. During their negotiations he points Zak toward the southern lizard islands and says their gold will be his. Zak can follow that lead, only to find hostile owners who pursue the intruder. When confronted, the Hermit calls the misleading invitation a joke, imitating the humour Zak had used with him. Zak makes clear that he does not find the experience amusing.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing l30",
							"briefing l30_1",
							"briefing l35",
							"quest q35g",
							"quest q35ag"
						],
						"event": "hermit_treasure",
						"order": 19
					},
					{
						"heading": "Water for the lizards and the Great Magician",
						"text": "The Hermit needs the water diverted by the orcs restored. Zak raises their dam and blocks the diversion canal; in return the lizard locates the magician in a ravine in the Remote Mountains. This information joins the tunnel instructions from the Khadaganian report. Zak passes the ancient defences and reaches [[character/rick]]. Tka-Rik explains that his dragonet has watched Zak since his unusual arrival. Bound to Gipath by his duty as its Great Magician, he offers help with Zak’s memory in exchange for bringing him two surviving young Joons. The first journey will be to Ingos to find the girl. Zak must leave his Gipath companions behind. Tka-Rik supplies an amulet for the return journey, but warns that it will work only once the girl has been found; his dragonet will travel with Zak.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing l30",
							"quest q34g",
							"briefing l34",
							"briefing z35",
							"quest q36g",
							"briefing r36",
							"briefing r36_3",
							"briefing r36_4",
							"briefing r36_2"
						],
						"event": "tka_rik",
						"order": 20
					},
					{
						"heading": "The world beyond Gipath",
						"text": "Tka-Rik places Gipath’s isolation in a longer history. Before the Sundering it belonged to a continent; the Joon civilisation and later states have left successive layers of ruins. The Night of Troubles destroyed many of those later realms. Cania and Khadagan survived, but Tka-Rik distinguishes their societies: he regards Cania as comparatively free, while warning that Khadagan subordinates everyone to someone stronger. His account gives Zak a wider setting for the local ruins and conflicts.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r36_1",
							"briefing r54_1"
						],
						"event": "rick_world",
						"order": 21
					},
					{
						"heading": "The rescue of Airy",
						"text": "Before leaving Gipath, Zak can finish the separate search for Airy, who set out to find the magician herself and impress him. Estera’s vision points to a goblin prison, where Zak finds her granddaughter’s potion bottle and a note leading to [[character/orcc]]. The orc chief intends to give Airy to a frightening armoured human he calls a Demon. Zak offers to remove this threat instead. Shei-Var first demands proof of his strength against the ogre brothers, whose wealth also threatens the chief’s influence. Once Zak defeats the Demon, Shei-Var honours his promise and sends Airy home. The rescue ends with Estera’s gratitude and Airy’s distress that Zak still regards her as a child.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z38",
							"briefing z39",
							"briefing sh40",
							"briefing sh41",
							"briefing sh41_1",
							"briefing z41_1",
							"quest q39g",
							"quest q41g"
						],
						"event": "airy_rescue",
						"order": 22
					},
					{
						"heading": "At-Zako: the search turns back on itself",
						"text": "Tka-Rik initially expects Zak to bring the missing Joon with him. Lu-a-Jalla defends the exhausted traveller, and Zak insists that he will continue for her sake even if reunion with At-Zako means losing her. The amulet changes everything: Tka-Rik discovers that its destination was Gipath. Only Zak arrived there in that unusual way. The man he has been seeking is himself, hidden by the Khadaganian transformation spell. Restored as At-Zako, he reassures Tka-Rik and reunites with Lu-a-Jalla. The memories of their earlier flight explain why the return of his identity also brings back the danger of the Curse. If companions have come from Suslanger, Tka-Rik sends them back by a safe route beyond the imperial guards. Their departure does not mean that they have settled on Gipath.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r72",
							"briefing r72_1",
							"briefing r72_4",
							"briefing d72_5",
							"briefing r72_3"
						],
						"event": "identity",
						"order": 37
					},
					{
						"heading": "The battle with the Joon Curse",
						"text": "The hurried transformation that concealed At-Zako nearly destroyed his memory. Tka-Rik cannot safely repeat it, so hiding as Zak is no longer an answer. He offers another choice: flee again under his temporary protection, or confront the Curse on Gipath. At-Zako chooses to defend his life with Lu-a-Jalla. Tka-Rik supplies ancient Joon equipment and joins him in the catacombs for the final battle. Defeating the Curse completes the campaign.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r72_6",
							"briefing r72_7",
							"quest q73g",
							"zone gz20g"
						],
						"event": "curse",
						"order": 38
					}
				],
				"name_key": "allod gipat",
				"id": "gipat"
			},
			"allod/ingos": {
				"kind": "allod",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Ingos belongs to Cania. Its thinly populated provinces leave room for independent communities and the Woodsfolk. A governor and city guards represent imperial authority, but the original gazetteer stresses the inhabitants’ independence. Snowy country, mines, forests and older underground ruins surround the city.",
						"sources": [
							"allod ingos",
							"zone gz11k",
							"zone gz12k"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Arrival on Ingos and the missing Princess",
						"text": "On [[allod/ingos]], Zak first crosses the hostile countryside to reach the city. [[character/kapitan]] tells him about the missing young woman known locally as the Princess and the reward offered for finding her. Governor [[character/huber]] takes a personal interest in her, while [[character/magess]] discourages the search. The hunter [[character/trapper]] provides a concrete lead: another investigator, the Snoop from the capital, had followed the same trail before disappearing east of the city. Finding his body changes the search for a missing girl into an investigation of murder.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q42k",
							"briefing k42",
							"briefing k42_1",
							"briefing g42",
							"briefing mg42",
							"briefing t42",
							"briefing k43"
						],
						"event": "ingos_arrival",
						"order": 23
					},
					{
						"heading": "The arrow and the Green Clan",
						"text": "The arrow in the Snoop’s body appears to implicate the Woodsfolk. Sver explains that their markings identify a clan, while the governor discovers that the case containing similar arrows has been stolen from his collection. Two lines of inquiry now run alongside each other: the stolen collection and the Woodsfolk’s testimony. To approach [[character/ycf]], Zak first returns the Green Clan’s Sacred Flower after rescuing its keeper from the White Clan. Grey Leaf then asks for help with the kidnapped elder Khabburu and his captor. The clan ultimately identifies the murder arrow as a false attribution, even refusing to blame its enemies for it. When the clan moves, Zak receives access to its hidden route and new home.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing g43",
							"briefing t43",
							"briefing yc45",
							"briefing yc46",
							"briefing yc47",
							"briefing yc47_1"
						],
						"event": "arrow",
						"order": 24
					},
					{
						"heading": "Karansul’s accounts and Baldie’s confession",
						"text": "[[character/shopper]] first lets Baldie’s name slip while boasting that mechanical traps are better than magical ones, then denies knowing anything useful. Tilvar mentions the merchant’s mechanical grinder and the disruption caused when it breaks. Sver knows how to turn these leads into pressure. Zak first delivers the hunter’s furs to a buyer outside the merchant’s monopoly. Sver then reveals Karansul’s hidden trade and unpaid taxes, pointing to the foreman’s accounts; he also recommends [[character/mthief]] as a teacher. With the accounts as leverage, Zak learns where to find [[character/vor]], called Baldie, and secures a meeting. Evidence from the stolen collection makes the thief confess: he killed the Snoop, planted the Woodsfolk arrow and worked for a cloaked employer who knew how to evade the governor’s magical traps.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing t43_1",
							"briefing t44",
							"briefing mthief1",
							"briefing tr48",
							"briefing tr49",
							"briefing tr49_1",
							"briefing tr43_1",
							"briefing k44"
						],
						"event": "karansul",
						"order": 25
					},
					{
						"heading": "Diera and the locked mines",
						"text": "At the Green Clan’s new home, Grey Leaf describes the supposed man in grey as a white-haired woman. Zak identifies Diera, whose own traps protected the stolen collection. Her urgent warnings against the abandoned mines reinforce his suspicion. Tilvar explains that the governor holds the key. Karansul suggests winning it with a gold nugget and gives access to a dangerous pit, concealing the tigers that drove out its workers; Sver warns Zak of the trap. Zak obtains the nugget and presents it to the collector-governor as a business prospect. Valrasian releases the mine key, opening the way past Diera’s mercenaries.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing yc50",
							"briefing mg43_1",
							"briefing mg50",
							"briefing k51",
							"briefing tr52",
							"briefing t52",
							"briefing g53",
							"quest q54k"
						],
						"event": "diera",
						"order": 26
					},
					{
						"heading": "Lu-a-Jalla beneath the mines",
						"text": "The former punishment cell is empty. Zak follows the trail deeper into the Forbidden Catacombs until he finds [[character/deva]], who first mistakes him for another captor. She has escaped the mercenaries and found protection through the ancient treaty between Joons and Wormheads. She rejects the nickname Princess and explains that Diera imprisoned her out of jealousy over the governor. Zak falls in love with her, but she remains faithful to At-Zako, who drew the Joon Curse away from her. Zak promises to respect that bond. They return to Tka-Rik, and the next mission becomes the search for her missing beloved on Suslanger.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q55k",
							"briefing d55",
							"briefing d55_1",
							"briefing d55_3",
							"briefing r54"
						],
						"event": "lua",
						"order": 27
					}
				],
				"name_key": "allod ingos",
				"id": "ingos"
			},
			"allod/suslanger": {
				"kind": "allod",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Suslanger is a dry Khadaganian allod with little cultivable land. A river, lake, dams and irrigation channels sustain its settlements. Imperial decrees are enforced by guards and informers. Beyond that order lie necromancers’ laboratories, Wormface caverns and the hidden refuge of escaped slaves.",
						"sources": [
							"allod suslanger",
							"zone gz16h",
							"zone gz17h"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Tka-Rik’s warning about Suslanger",
						"text": "Before Zak leaves for Suslanger, Tka-Rik describes the Khadaganian hierarchy, in which even a master is subject to someone above him. Emperor Neseb is himself a powerful magician. Suslanger is a provincial backwater, but that does not make it safe. The old Joon portal provides a route there; informants and imperial attention make open enquiries about the missing Joon dangerous.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r54_1",
							"briefing r54_2"
						],
						"event": "suslanger_warning",
						"order": 28
					},
					{
						"heading": "Captivity in Death Canyon",
						"text": "Zak is captured near the portal on [[allod/suslanger]]. Lieutenant [[character/karh]] presents him to [[character/iz]], who orders him used as live quarry for soldiers seeking promotion. Surviving the run through Death Canyon earns only a return to captivity, not freedom. Kardaur threatens punishment through necromancy if Zak dies too quickly and displeases his superior.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing hns59",
							"quest q60h"
						],
						"event": "canyon",
						"order": 29
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Odalisque and the escape",
						"text": "The [[character/nalo]] approaches Zak after his ordeal. Enslaved by Kardaur and deprived even of a personal name, she wants his death and knows he will guard the gate that night. She steals the key to Zak’s cage. Her father, a rat hunter who disappeared in the caves, told her of the Wormheads’ temple and the bridges across its lava rivers. She uses that knowledge to explain a route the guards fear to follow. Zak kills Kardaur for the fortress key and escapes toward the cave; releasing the caged spiders can delay pursuit. The Odalisque and Zak part with wishes for each other’s freedom and survival, without a stated account of her later fate.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing kr60",
							"briefing kr61",
							"quest q61h",
							"quest q62h",
							"quest q63h"
						],
						"event": "odalisque",
						"order": 30
					},
					{
						"heading": "Acceptance into the Last Shelter",
						"text": "Beyond the caves, [[character/kuzn]] finds Zak and vouches for him before [[character/glav]]. The brotherhood’s leader fears imperial spies and requires the Emperor’s Signet Ring, taken from a cyclops who killed its courier. Possessing such an object compromises the newcomer with the authorities and makes the test more than a feat of strength. After Zak returns, Khatul accepts him as Brother Blondie and offers the refuge as a new home. Zak explains his promise to find At-Zako and return to Gipath. Khatul respects that purpose and asks those who can help to support him; [[character/guru]] can teach him greater dexterity.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing gl63",
							"briefing gl64",
							"briefing guru1"
						],
						"event": "shelter",
						"order": 31
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Blacksmith’s revenge and Kehel’s experiment",
						"text": "The Shelter’s inhabitants can ask for help with their own grievances alongside the search for At-Zako. Brother Blacksmith wants the slavedriver brothers Lash-Aga and Whip-Aga killed at the plantations. If Zak carries out the request, the workers raise the alarm instead of helping him. The smith explains that imperial law threatens them with death if they fail to report a stranger, then promises to repay the revenge. Kehel offers a separate commission: destroy a harpy he once experimented on, so rivals cannot claim his work. After its death, he admits that fear of Zak’s strength is what makes him honour the promised reward. Their requests expose different consequences of life under the empire: coerced obedience among the workers, personal revenge among its fugitives, and rivalry among its scholars.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing kuz64_2",
							"briefing kuz64_3",
							"briefing ne64_2",
							"briefing ne64_3",
							"quest q64ah",
							"quest qk31h"
						],
						"event": "shelter_revenge",
						"order": 32
					},
					{
						"heading": "Sahilia’s cache and the Joon collector",
						"text": "[[character/merc10]] supplies the next lead through her own theft. Antiquities she stole from [[character/junofil]] remain hidden near the city, and Zak recovers them to arrange a meeting. Khatul and Brother Blacksmith help choose the secluded trading place, keeping strangers away from the Shelter. Baluddin admits recognising At-Zako as a living Joon and wanting to keep him in his own house. Imperial scholars took the young man away. Later, Taivo summoned the collector to identify those scholars’ mutilated bodies. The missing youth’s trail now leads to the necromancers rather than a living prisoner whom Zak can simply rescue.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing vo64",
							"briefing vo65",
							"briefing gl66",
							"briefing jf67",
							"quest q65h",
							"quest q66h",
							"quest q67h"
						],
						"event": "collector",
						"order": 33
					},
					{
						"heading": "Kholai and the dead scientist",
						"text": "[[character/nekro]] directs Zak to [[character/oldnekro]], whom Taivo displaced from the imperial laboratory. Kholai wants disgrace for his rival rather than a simple assassination. Zak steals a dead scientist’s head from the laboratory; its disappearance will embarrass Taivo, and Kholai can summon its spirit. The [[character/spirit|scientist]] describes a horror pursuing the red-skinned youth. To conceal him, the scholars changed his appearance and sent him away by teleportation. Kholai interprets the broken testimony and tells Zak to search the ruined mansion for the magical object used in the escape. The fragments Zak finds are beyond Kehel’s ability to interpret: only a Great Magician can identify the destination. Kholai can also give Zak an experimental object to test in combat. He is interested in the result even if the trial kills its user, a further sign of the limits of his concern for his visitor.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing ne67",
							"briefing sn67",
							"quest q69h",
							"briefing sn69",
							"briefing sn69_1",
							"quest q70h",
							"briefing ne70",
							"briefing sn67_1"
						],
						"event": "spirit",
						"order": 35
					},
					{
						"heading": "Brother Faceless and the return to Gipath",
						"text": "Khatul asks [[character/zaku]] to help Zak reach the guarded portal. Faceless arranges a cave meeting and explains the keys and stone controls. He then reveals himself as Erfar: born on Suslanger, he went to Gipath seeking the meteorite and manipulated the villagers into searching for it. His earlier plots were attempts to remove Zak, who disrupted that scheme. The reunion is another trap, with guards waiting outside the cave. Zak survives the confrontation and forces a route through the portal’s defences. Activating its two controls finally returns him to Tka-Rik with the amulet fragments.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s64",
							"briefing gl70",
							"briefing s71",
							"briefing s71_1",
							"quest q72h"
						],
						"event": "faceless",
						"order": 36
					}
				],
				"name_key": "allod suslanger",
				"id": "suslanger"
			},
			"map/bz1g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The village is Gipath’s only human settlement. Here Erfar names Zak the Chosen, Gort commands the guards and Babur offers his craft. It is both Zak’s first refuge and the centre of the local conspiracy he eventually exposes.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz1g",
							"briefing s1",
							"briefing s25_1"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The village and the Chosen",
						"text": "At the village assembly, [[character/elder]] identifies Zak with the promised Chosen who was to appear at the Sacred Ruins. [[character/sheriff]], the captain, supports the ceremony. The offer of food, shelter and honour comes with a threat: an impostor would be thrown into the fire. Zak accepts the title and gives his name. Erfar immediately sends him against the goblin sentry watching the village, reasoning that his plain clothes and unusual metal knife will let him approach where armed villagers cannot. Afterwards Zak learns that this is Gipath’s only human village, with no king or known route to other lands.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s1",
							"briefing s0",
							"briefing v0",
							"quest q1ag"
						],
						"event": "chosen",
						"order": 2
					},
					{
						"heading": "The wolf, the captain and the craftsman",
						"text": "Killing the sentry brings another request from Erfar: the White Wolf and its pack are taking the village livestock. After Zak kills their leader, Erfar and Gort press him to surrender the head so the captain can claim prestige. Gort also sends him to punish the goblins while the village soldiers stay home. Meanwhile [[character/smith]] refuses to work until his stolen iron tools are returned. Erfar calls the thieves friendly “Free Hunters”; Babur calls them brigands and describes the beating of his apprentice. Recovering the chisel and bodkin from the chief and his wife wins Zak the craftsman’s services. These early errands expose the disagreement between the village’s leaders and the man who equips it.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s1_3",
							"briefing s3",
							"briefing v3",
							"briefing v3_1",
							"briefing m1",
							"briefing m1_1",
							"briefing m4",
							"quest q2g"
						],
						"event": "village_errands",
						"order": 3
					},
					{
						"heading": "Two searches on the islands",
						"text": "The river islands hold two different leads. Estera needs the Orb, which Erfar places on the southernmost island. Erfar also proposes tracing [[character/bgoblinc]]’s blue Eye Stone to a possible portal. Zak takes the First Goblin’s skull to compel GoGo to visit the village; the chief explains that the orcs confiscated the written record of the stone’s origin. The islands require their own bridge mechanism, distinct from the cave’s. Zak retrieves the record and the Orb in this area. Erfar claims the record offers nothing useful, then diverts attention to missing taxes and gold in Golden Valley. His portal scheme and Estera’s search serve different purposes, even though they take Zak through the same territory.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s6_2",
							"briefing s7_1",
							"briefing s7_4",
							"briefing s8_2",
							"briefing v5",
							"quest q7g",
							"quest q8g",
							"quest q9g",
							"quest q10g"
						],
						"event": "orb_islands",
						"order": 8
					},
					{
						"heading": "Rival plans for the Eye Stone",
						"text": "GoGo’s presence in the village exposes competing interests. Gort silences the chief under Erfar’s orders and refuses to let Zak question him without the elder. Babur, meanwhile, recognises the Eye Stone as a valuable meteorite. He proposes killing GoGo and dividing the proceeds. Zak rejects the suggestion, making clear that his dealings with the craftsman do not amount to accepting every scheme he offers.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing gogo0",
							"briefing m7_1"
						],
						"event": "eye_stone_scheme",
						"order": 9
					},
					{
						"heading": "Golden Valley and the missing apprentice",
						"text": "Erfar links the missing village taxes to the disappearance of Babur’s apprentice and accuses the craftsman of plotting to take power. Babur denies the charge: he suspects the brigands of stealing the gold and kidnapping his worker. Erfar proposes replacing the money with treasure from a ruined caravan in Golden Valley. Babur exposes the danger omitted from this offer: earlier visitors vanished, and one survivor returned deranged, speaking of undead guards. Zak can refuse the expedition. Erfar then reproaches him and later claims another man supplied the gold. If Zak instead retrieves it, the elder thanks him with promises of a song and admits that he understated the danger. Neither exchange establishes what actually happened to the apprentice.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s8_2",
							"briefing m12",
							"briefing m12_1",
							"briefing s12",
							"briefing s12_1",
							"briefing s12_3",
							"quest q12g"
						],
						"event": "golden_valley",
						"order": 11
					},
					{
						"heading": "The supposed attack on the goblins",
						"text": "While Zak seeks the magician, Erfar and Gort arrange another joint operation with the Free Hunters, supposedly to prevent a goblin raid. The promised friendly meeting becomes an ambush by brigands and village soldiers. Erfar denies responsibility. Gort blames drunken, undisciplined soldiers and brigands dressed as villagers, then briefly threatens to overthrow Erfar before withdrawing the threat. Babur warns that the elder is trying to have Zak killed. A note in the newer orc script cannot yet settle the matter: Estera needs an Orc ABC Book to read it. Its translation reveals a contract on Zak’s life, a detailed description of him and a substantial payment. The search for the author becomes a separate investigation alongside the route into the Dead City.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s12_4",
							"briefing s13_0",
							"briefing m13_3",
							"briefing z13_2",
							"briefing z14",
							"quest q13ag",
							"quest q14g",
							"briefing v13_0",
							"briefing v13_1",
							"briefing v13_2"
						],
						"event": "ambush",
						"order": 12
					},
					{
						"heading": "Gort’s rebellion",
						"text": "The written instructions to kill Zak expose Gort’s handwriting. Confronted with the evidence, the captain turns against the authority he formerly served and declares that he will rule the village himself. He flees to gather the forest men. Erfar urges Zak to stop him before he can return; the ensuing pursuit ends Gort’s rebellion. [[character/nsheriff]] becomes the new captain. Unlike his predecessor, Rivar admits that he dislikes fighting, shares his family’s secret of strength and later supports Zak when suspicion finally reaches the elder.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing v19",
							"briefing v19_1",
							"briefing m21",
							"briefing s21",
							"briefing nv23",
							"briefing nv26",
							"quest q19g",
							"quest q23g"
						],
						"event": "gort",
						"order": 15
					},
					{
						"heading": "The White Flower and Erfar’s exposure",
						"text": "After Gort’s fall, Erfar claims that an orc seer has cursed the village with disease and asks for a White Flower. Babur wants to monopolise the healing flower and charge Erfar ruinous prices; Zak rebukes him. Zak finds armed opposition waiting for him, although no villagers have yet fallen ill. Erfar blames Babur, while Babur’s earlier warning makes Zak suspect both men. Estera suggests setting a trap. Zak tells each suspect a different location for a second flower; the ambush appears at the place he gave Erfar. With Rivar’s support he confronts and imprisons the elder. An expedition report recovered from the abandoned foreign camp becomes leverage: Erfar translates its account of a concealed tunnel and its opening lever. He later escapes custody. Estera recalls that he originally arrived as an adult stranger, leaving his real purpose unresolved. Babur welcomes the exposure and says that the villagers feared Erfar rather than loved him.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing s21_1",
							"briefing s24",
							"briefing m24",
							"briefing z24_1",
							"quest q25g",
							"briefing nv26",
							"briefing s25_1",
							"briefing s25_3",
							"briefing nv27_1",
							"briefing z25",
							"quest q26g",
							"briefing m21_1",
							"briefing m26"
						],
						"event": "erfar_exposed",
						"order": 17
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz1g",
				"id": "bz1g"
			},
			"map/bz2g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Estera’s secluded cave is a place of advice, bargaining and magical instruction. The witch helps Zak investigate his missing memory, while Airy and Naya connect the cave with the wider life of the village.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz2g",
							"briefing z1",
							"briefing n8_3"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Meeting Estera and Airy",
						"text": "Before Estera’s formal introduction, Zak can meet Airy at the cave; she warns him about her grandmother and the traps protecting their home. [[character/witch]] initially threatens the intruder, then changes her reception when Zak offers the Magic Bit. She dismisses the villagers’ divine-saviour story but discovers that she cannot see his past. Since Zak cannot remember it either, she suggests finding the orcs’ Magic Orb and directs him back to Erfar for information. This is the beginning of their alliance. Estera also introduces her granddaughter [[character/daughter]], warning Zak to keep his distance; Airy resents being treated as a child. Zak’s search now has a personal aim beyond the village’s demands.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z1",
							"briefing z1_2",
							"briefing z0_2",
							"briefing m6_1",
							"briefing z0",
							"briefing z0_1"
						],
						"event": "estera",
						"order": 6
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Orb and the Dead City",
						"text": "When Estera uses the Orb, Zak sees an old man instead of a recognisable memory. She believes he is its owner and a more powerful magician who might help. Orc records trace the object to the Dead City. Estera distinguishes its human fortress from Gipath’s much older ruins: after the Night of Troubles, the dead attacked the living, and survivors fled to found the present village. The obvious route into the fortress is deadly. Airy leaves to investigate another way in, despite her grandmother’s objections. Separately, Erfar can send Zak into the surrounding region’s Golden Valley for a caravan’s lost treasure, an errand he badly understates.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z2",
							"briefing z11",
							"briefing z11_1",
							"briefing s8_2",
							"briefing s12",
							"briefing s12_1",
							"quest q12g"
						],
						"event": "dead_city_lead",
						"order": 10
					},
					{
						"heading": "Airy’s route through the observatory",
						"text": "Airy returns with a lead to the old observatory library, but harpies occupy the site. Her plan requires a lizard’s amulet to lead a particular green dragon against them. Reaching that dragon requires Estera to freeze the lake with a green crystal. The hostile lizards on the approach present another obstacle: Zak moves the Big Shell from the Remote Mountains to the Middle Mountains, restoring the peace associated with its altar. This leaves Babur’s former quarry route unsafe and can lead to an additional errand for the craftsman. The observatory book finally supplies the password to the Dead City’s hidden entrance. Airy can also teach Zak the Magic of Feelings, only to be offended when he again calls her a little girl.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z13_1",
							"briefing z14_1",
							"briefing m13_2",
							"briefing m15",
							"briefing z17",
							"briefing z15",
							"briefing z18",
							"quest q15ag",
							"quest q15bg",
							"quest q18g"
						],
						"event": "observatory",
						"order": 13
					},
					{
						"heading": "The expedition and the Hermit’s bargain",
						"text": "Estera introduces a lost Khadaganian [[character/clerk|official]], who promises that his expedition can help Zak leave Gipath. Meeting [[character/hwleader]] instead leads to imprisonment and demands for information about a meteorite. Zak plays the ignorant swineherd, but the commander threatens torture. [[character/oliz]] offers escape on the condition that Zak later visit his home. Zak gives his word, receives the Ancient Claw for the traps on the approach and escapes. The Hermit explains that he had intended to negotiate with the armoured humans but changed his mind after seeing their treatment of Zak. Keeping the promise establishes trust where the expedition failed to do so.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z27",
							"briefing ha29",
							"briefing ha29_1",
							"briefing ha29_2",
							"briefing ha29_3",
							"briefing l30"
						],
						"event": "expedition",
						"order": 18
					},
					{
						"heading": "The rescue of Airy",
						"text": "Before leaving Gipath, Zak can finish the separate search for Airy, who set out to find the magician herself and impress him. Estera’s vision points to a goblin prison, where Zak finds her granddaughter’s potion bottle and a note leading to [[character/orcc]]. The orc chief intends to give Airy to a frightening armoured human he calls a Demon. Zak offers to remove this threat instead. Shei-Var first demands proof of his strength against the ogre brothers, whose wealth also threatens the chief’s influence. Once Zak defeats the Demon, Shei-Var honours his promise and sends Airy home. The rescue ends with Estera’s gratitude and Airy’s distress that Zak still regards her as a child.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z38",
							"briefing z39",
							"briefing sh40",
							"briefing sh41",
							"briefing sh41_1",
							"briefing z41_1",
							"quest q39g",
							"quest q41g"
						],
						"event": "airy_rescue",
						"order": 22
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz2g",
				"id": "bz2g"
			},
			"map/bz3g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Old Dragon’s dwelling brings Zak face to face with a powerful, knowledgeable creature. The dragon’s information forms part of the search for the Great Magician; the meeting is a negotiation, not merely a monster encounter.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz3g",
							"briefing dr22"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Old Dragon on Lone Mountain",
						"text": "The observatory’s password takes Zak into the Dead City and up Lone Mountain. He expects the magician from the Orb but finds [[character/skd]]. The dragon insists on an old bargain: a visitor must perform a task before receiving an answer. Zak deals with the armadillos beneath the lair, using the poison the dragon supplies. The dragon then admits that he cannot identify Zak or locate Gipath’s elusive Great Magician. He suggests the ancient lizards might know more and points instead to an expedition of armoured outsiders. He withdraws the skeletons he had used against their camp. In a further conversation, he gives Zak an old sword because he enjoys his company and would rather not see him become another speechless skeleton.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q20g",
							"briefing dr20",
							"quest q22g",
							"briefing dr22",
							"briefing dr22_1"
						],
						"event": "dragon",
						"order": 16
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz3g",
				"id": "bz3g"
			},
			"map/bz4g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Khadaganian expedition has established a foothold on Gipath in pursuit of the meteorite. Zak’s capture connects its officers, the Hermit Lizard and the struggle over the island’s secrets.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz4g",
							"briefing ha29",
							"briefing l30"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The expedition and the Hermit’s bargain",
						"text": "Estera introduces a lost Khadaganian [[character/clerk|official]], who promises that his expedition can help Zak leave Gipath. Meeting [[character/hwleader]] instead leads to imprisonment and demands for information about a meteorite. Zak plays the ignorant swineherd, but the commander threatens torture. [[character/oliz]] offers escape on the condition that Zak later visit his home. Zak gives his word, receives the Ancient Claw for the traps on the approach and escapes. The Hermit explains that he had intended to negotiate with the armoured humans but changed his mind after seeing their treatment of Zak. Keeping the promise establishes trust where the expedition failed to do so.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z27",
							"briefing ha29",
							"briefing ha29_1",
							"briefing ha29_2",
							"briefing ha29_3",
							"briefing l30"
						],
						"event": "expedition",
						"order": 18
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz4g",
				"id": "bz4g"
			},
			"map/bz5g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Hermit Lizard’s home is a refuge with its own urgent problem: access to water. His bargain with Zak links a rescue, the orcs’ dam and the difficult route toward the Great Magician.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz5g",
							"briefing l34"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The expedition and the Hermit’s bargain",
						"text": "Estera introduces a lost Khadaganian [[character/clerk|official]], who promises that his expedition can help Zak leave Gipath. Meeting [[character/hwleader]] instead leads to imprisonment and demands for information about a meteorite. Zak plays the ignorant swineherd, but the commander threatens torture. [[character/oliz]] offers escape on the condition that Zak later visit his home. Zak gives his word, receives the Ancient Claw for the traps on the approach and escapes. The Hermit explains that he had intended to negotiate with the armoured humans but changed his mind after seeing their treatment of Zak. Keeping the promise establishes trust where the expedition failed to do so.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z27",
							"briefing ha29",
							"briefing ha29_1",
							"briefing ha29_2",
							"briefing ha29_3",
							"briefing l30"
						],
						"event": "expedition",
						"order": 18
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Hermit’s gift and dangerous joke",
						"text": "The Hermit offers more than directions. He gives Zak an object resembling a horn or shell, hoping it will help him survive, although he cannot explain how a human should use it. His advice about treasure is less benevolent. During their negotiations he points Zak toward the southern lizard islands and says their gold will be his. Zak can follow that lead, only to find hostile owners who pursue the intruder. When confronted, the Hermit calls the misleading invitation a joke, imitating the humour Zak had used with him. Zak makes clear that he does not find the experience amusing.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing l30",
							"briefing l30_1",
							"briefing l35",
							"quest q35g",
							"quest q35ag"
						],
						"event": "hermit_treasure",
						"order": 19
					},
					{
						"heading": "Water for the lizards and the Great Magician",
						"text": "The Hermit needs the water diverted by the orcs restored. Zak raises their dam and blocks the diversion canal; in return the lizard locates the magician in a ravine in the Remote Mountains. This information joins the tunnel instructions from the Khadaganian report. Zak passes the ancient defences and reaches [[character/rick]]. Tka-Rik explains that his dragonet has watched Zak since his unusual arrival. Bound to Gipath by his duty as its Great Magician, he offers help with Zak’s memory in exchange for bringing him two surviving young Joons. The first journey will be to Ingos to find the girl. Zak must leave his Gipath companions behind. Tka-Rik supplies an amulet for the return journey, but warns that it will work only once the girl has been found; his dragonet will travel with Zak.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing l30",
							"quest q34g",
							"briefing l34",
							"briefing z35",
							"quest q36g",
							"briefing r36",
							"briefing r36_3",
							"briefing r36_4",
							"briefing r36_2"
						],
						"event": "tka_rik",
						"order": 20
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz5g",
				"id": "bz5g"
			},
			"map/bz6g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Shei-Var’s settlement is an orc power centre in the Sands. Airy’s captivity and the chief’s ritual plans bring Zak here. The settlement also offers a different perspective on village life through Eila, a human who chose to learn magic among the orcs.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz6g",
							"briefing sh40",
							"briefing n8_4"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The rescue of Airy",
						"text": "Before leaving Gipath, Zak can finish the separate search for Airy, who set out to find the magician herself and impress him. Estera’s vision points to a goblin prison, where Zak finds her granddaughter’s potion bottle and a note leading to [[character/orcc]]. The orc chief intends to give Airy to a frightening armoured human he calls a Demon. Zak offers to remove this threat instead. Shei-Var first demands proof of his strength against the ogre brothers, whose wealth also threatens the chief’s influence. Once Zak defeats the Demon, Shei-Var honours his promise and sends Airy home. The rescue ends with Estera’s gratitude and Airy’s distress that Zak still regards her as a child.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing z38",
							"briefing z39",
							"briefing sh40",
							"briefing sh41",
							"briefing sh41_1",
							"briefing z41_1",
							"quest q39g",
							"quest q41g"
						],
						"event": "airy_rescue",
						"order": 22
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz6g",
				"id": "bz6g"
			},
			"map/bz7g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Tka-Rik’s underground dwelling is the turning point between Zak’s local search and his journey across the allods. Here the magician asks him to find surviving Joons, receives his discoveries and finally helps reveal the truth about At-Zako.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz7g",
							"briefing r36",
							"briefing r54",
							"briefing r72_4"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Water for the lizards and the Great Magician",
						"text": "The Hermit needs the water diverted by the orcs restored. Zak raises their dam and blocks the diversion canal; in return the lizard locates the magician in a ravine in the Remote Mountains. This information joins the tunnel instructions from the Khadaganian report. Zak passes the ancient defences and reaches [[character/rick]]. Tka-Rik explains that his dragonet has watched Zak since his unusual arrival. Bound to Gipath by his duty as its Great Magician, he offers help with Zak’s memory in exchange for bringing him two surviving young Joons. The first journey will be to Ingos to find the girl. Zak must leave his Gipath companions behind. Tka-Rik supplies an amulet for the return journey, but warns that it will work only once the girl has been found; his dragonet will travel with Zak.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing l30",
							"quest q34g",
							"briefing l34",
							"briefing z35",
							"quest q36g",
							"briefing r36",
							"briefing r36_3",
							"briefing r36_4",
							"briefing r36_2"
						],
						"event": "tka_rik",
						"order": 20
					},
					{
						"heading": "The world beyond Gipath",
						"text": "Tka-Rik places Gipath’s isolation in a longer history. Before the Sundering it belonged to a continent; the Joon civilisation and later states have left successive layers of ruins. The Night of Troubles destroyed many of those later realms. Cania and Khadagan survived, but Tka-Rik distinguishes their societies: he regards Cania as comparatively free, while warning that Khadagan subordinates everyone to someone stronger. His account gives Zak a wider setting for the local ruins and conflicts.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r36_1",
							"briefing r54_1"
						],
						"event": "rick_world",
						"order": 21
					},
					{
						"heading": "Lu-a-Jalla beneath the mines",
						"text": "The former punishment cell is empty. Zak follows the trail deeper into the Forbidden Catacombs until he finds [[character/deva]], who first mistakes him for another captor. She has escaped the mercenaries and found protection through the ancient treaty between Joons and Wormheads. She rejects the nickname Princess and explains that Diera imprisoned her out of jealousy over the governor. Zak falls in love with her, but she remains faithful to At-Zako, who drew the Joon Curse away from her. Zak promises to respect that bond. They return to Tka-Rik, and the next mission becomes the search for her missing beloved on Suslanger.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q55k",
							"briefing d55",
							"briefing d55_1",
							"briefing d55_3",
							"briefing r54"
						],
						"event": "lua",
						"order": 27
					},
					{
						"heading": "Tka-Rik’s warning about Suslanger",
						"text": "Before Zak leaves for Suslanger, Tka-Rik describes the Khadaganian hierarchy, in which even a master is subject to someone above him. Emperor Neseb is himself a powerful magician. Suslanger is a provincial backwater, but that does not make it safe. The old Joon portal provides a route there; informants and imperial attention make open enquiries about the missing Joon dangerous.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r54_1",
							"briefing r54_2"
						],
						"event": "suslanger_warning",
						"order": 28
					},
					{
						"heading": "At-Zako: the search turns back on itself",
						"text": "Tka-Rik initially expects Zak to bring the missing Joon with him. Lu-a-Jalla defends the exhausted traveller, and Zak insists that he will continue for her sake even if reunion with At-Zako means losing her. The amulet changes everything: Tka-Rik discovers that its destination was Gipath. Only Zak arrived there in that unusual way. The man he has been seeking is himself, hidden by the Khadaganian transformation spell. Restored as At-Zako, he reassures Tka-Rik and reunites with Lu-a-Jalla. The memories of their earlier flight explain why the return of his identity also brings back the danger of the Curse. If companions have come from Suslanger, Tka-Rik sends them back by a safe route beyond the imperial guards. Their departure does not mean that they have settled on Gipath.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r72",
							"briefing r72_1",
							"briefing r72_4",
							"briefing d72_5",
							"briefing r72_3"
						],
						"event": "identity",
						"order": 37
					},
					{
						"heading": "The battle with the Joon Curse",
						"text": "The hurried transformation that concealed At-Zako nearly destroyed his memory. Tka-Rik cannot safely repeat it, so hiding as Zak is no longer an answer. He offers another choice: flee again under his temporary protection, or confront the Curse on Gipath. At-Zako chooses to defend his life with Lu-a-Jalla. Tka-Rik supplies ancient Joon equipment and joins him in the catacombs for the final battle. Defeating the Curse completes the campaign.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing r72_6",
							"briefing r72_7",
							"quest q73g",
							"zone gz20g"
						],
						"event": "curse",
						"order": 38
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz7g",
				"id": "bz7g"
			},
			"map/bz8k": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The city concentrates Ingos’ official power and commerce. The governor, his captain and court magician shape Zak’s search for the Princess, while merchants and potential companions provide leads and assistance.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz8k",
							"briefing r36",
							"briefing n8_5",
							"briefing n8_6"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Arrival on Ingos and the missing Princess",
						"text": "On [[allod/ingos]], Zak first crosses the hostile countryside to reach the city. [[character/kapitan]] tells him about the missing young woman known locally as the Princess and the reward offered for finding her. Governor [[character/huber]] takes a personal interest in her, while [[character/magess]] discourages the search. The hunter [[character/trapper]] provides a concrete lead: another investigator, the Snoop from the capital, had followed the same trail before disappearing east of the city. Finding his body changes the search for a missing girl into an investigation of murder.",
						"sources": [
							"quest q42k",
							"briefing k42",
							"briefing k42_1",
							"briefing g42",
							"briefing mg42",
							"briefing t42",
							"briefing k43"
						],
						"event": "ingos_arrival",
						"order": 23
					},
					{
						"heading": "The arrow and the Green Clan",
						"text": "The arrow in the Snoop’s body appears to implicate the Woodsfolk. Sver explains that their markings identify a clan, while the governor discovers that the case containing similar arrows has been stolen from his collection. Two lines of inquiry now run alongside each other: the stolen collection and the Woodsfolk’s testimony. To approach [[character/ycf]], Zak first returns the Green Clan’s Sacred Flower after rescuing its keeper from the White Clan. Grey Leaf then asks for help with the kidnapped elder Khabburu and his captor. The clan ultimately identifies the murder arrow as a false attribution, even refusing to blame its enemies for it. When the clan moves, Zak receives access to its hidden route and new home.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing g43",
							"briefing t43",
							"briefing yc45",
							"briefing yc46",
							"briefing yc47",
							"briefing yc47_1"
						],
						"event": "arrow",
						"order": 24
					},
					{
						"heading": "Karansul’s accounts and Baldie’s confession",
						"text": "[[character/shopper]] first lets Baldie’s name slip while boasting that mechanical traps are better than magical ones, then denies knowing anything useful. Tilvar mentions the merchant’s mechanical grinder and the disruption caused when it breaks. Sver knows how to turn these leads into pressure. Zak first delivers the hunter’s furs to a buyer outside the merchant’s monopoly. Sver then reveals Karansul’s hidden trade and unpaid taxes, pointing to the foreman’s accounts; he also recommends [[character/mthief]] as a teacher. With the accounts as leverage, Zak learns where to find [[character/vor]], called Baldie, and secures a meeting. Evidence from the stolen collection makes the thief confess: he killed the Snoop, planted the Woodsfolk arrow and worked for a cloaked employer who knew how to evade the governor’s magical traps.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing t43_1",
							"briefing t44",
							"briefing mthief1",
							"briefing tr48",
							"briefing tr49",
							"briefing tr49_1",
							"briefing tr43_1",
							"briefing k44"
						],
						"event": "karansul",
						"order": 25
					},
					{
						"heading": "Diera and the locked mines",
						"text": "At the Green Clan’s new home, Grey Leaf describes the supposed man in grey as a white-haired woman. Zak identifies Diera, whose own traps protected the stolen collection. Her urgent warnings against the abandoned mines reinforce his suspicion. Tilvar explains that the governor holds the key. Karansul suggests winning it with a gold nugget and gives access to a dangerous pit, concealing the tigers that drove out its workers; Sver warns Zak of the trap. Zak obtains the nugget and presents it to the collector-governor as a business prospect. Valrasian releases the mine key, opening the way past Diera’s mercenaries.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing yc50",
							"briefing mg43_1",
							"briefing mg50",
							"briefing k51",
							"briefing tr52",
							"briefing t52",
							"briefing g53",
							"quest q54k"
						],
						"event": "diera",
						"order": 26
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz8k",
				"id": "bz8k"
			},
			"map/bz9k": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Green Clan’s home introduces Zak to the Woodsfolk and their distrust of outsiders. Their troubles, including the fate of their elder, become part of the search through Ingos.",
