							"briefing brief_52",
							"briefing brief_53",
							"briefing brief_70",
							"briefing brief_71",
							"briefing brief_72"
						],
						"event": "brainripper",
						"order": 10
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz4h",
				"id": "bz4h"
			},
			"map/bz5h": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The black market brings together craftspeople, illicit trade and local information. Master Arkhar and the fortune-teller offer very different services here. For conspicuous fugitives such as Kir and Kel, useful contacts are as valuable as equipment.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz5h",
							"briefing nsheriff_about",
							"briefing skelet_about"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The meteorite and Feiruz’s favour",
						"text": "Rindeil wants a meteorite sample brought from the lands the Khadaganians call useless. He has arranged to exchange it for a rare addition to his collection. Kir overhears the guards to locate the sample, but its locked chest calls for a better thief. Karim directs him to Feiruz at the Black Market. Kel challenges Feiruz’s pride until he agrees to help, on condition that they return the favour later. He opens the chest, earns Kel’s praise and leaves the party to rest. Rindeil rewards the delivery. The unpaid favour explains why Feiruz later expects help rescuing Aliya.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_18",
							"briefing brief_20",
							"briefing brief_22",
							"briefing brief_23",
							"briefing brief_24",
							"briefing brief_25",
							"quest q5h",
							"quest q7h",
							"quest q8h"
						],
						"event": "meteorite_theft",
						"order": 5
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz5h",
				"id": "bz5h"
			},
			"map/bz8g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Urk-Tar’s fortress is the centre of Kir’s dealings with the orcs. The chief and shaman connect his arrival with memories of Zak, while ritual obligations, a kidnapped wife and conflict with human settlers make the stronghold a place of competing demands.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz8g",
							"briefing brief_101",
							"briefing brief_102",
							"briefing brief_110",
							"briefing brief_112"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Urk-Tar and the destroyed expedition",
						"text": "On Gipath, [[character/orcc]] says his warriors destroyed the Khadaganian expedition. He remembers how Zak’s defeat of a supposed Demon showed that armoured outsiders could be killed, and hopes Kir will become a Chosen for his own tribe. Kel proposes a false report with unpromising stone samples to prevent another imperial expedition from being sent. The shaman [[character/orcpyr]] distrusts strangers but makes Kir a ritual axe after he supplies suitable stone, and makes beads for Kel. Their welcome depends on helping the tribe, and Kir soon rescues the chief from a harpy and his wife from goblins.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_101",
							"briefing brief_102",
							"briefing brief_103",
							"briefing brief_104",
							"briefing brief_105",
							"briefing brief_106",
							"briefing brief_107",
							"briefing brief_108",
							"briefing brief_109",
							"briefing brief_110",
							"briefing brief_111"
						],
						"event": "urk_tar",
						"order": 14
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Shell and the war with the settlers",
						"text": "Urk-Tar explains that humans have driven orcs out of the Foothills and are advancing into the Middle Mountains. Kir removes the Shell whose presence restrains the lizards, initially refusing to fight humans himself. When Tynachey describes the killing of the tribe’s inhabitants, Kel argues for helping their hosts recover the settlement, and Kir agrees. The [[character/iz|Hermit Lizard]] then offers news of the former Chosen in exchange for placing the Shell by the Remote Mountains’ lake. Restoring it also frees hunters held by lizards. The Hermit directs Kir toward the Great Magician by a route different from Zak’s old tunnel approach.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_112",
							"briefing brief_113",
							"briefing brief_114",
							"briefing brief_115",
							"briefing brief_116",
							"briefing brief_117"
						],
						"event": "shell_war",
						"order": 15
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz8g",
				"id": "bz8g"
			},
			"map/bz11g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Free Hunters’ camp is led by Bor. It gives Kir a foothold among people who claim independence from the village, yet intervene in its conflicts. Babur’s arrival through a deceptive message exposes how readily the camp’s interests become personal schemes.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz11g",
							"briefing brief_121",
							"briefing brief_126a",
							"briefing brief_126b"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Free Hunters and Babur",
						"text": "A [[character/loozer|messenger]] invites the rescuers of the hunters to [[character/karh]]’s camp. Bor claims neutrality in the war, yet asks them to act against the village’s hunters and caravans. To question [[character/smith]] about the Orb, Kir helps bring him out of the village. The letter can go to Babur himself or to the elder, with different explanations for the craftsman’s arrival. Babur then sends Kir to the lizards’ tomb for a supposed magical needle. The expedition supplies lizard hides, and Babur admits that useful armour material was his real interest. For information about the Orb, he redirects the visitors to Estera.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_120",
							"briefing brief_121",
							"briefing brief_123",
							"briefing brief_124",
							"briefing brief_126a",
							"briefing brief_126b",
							"briefing brief_127",
							"briefing brief_128"
						],
						"event": "bor_babur",
						"order": 17
					},
					{
						"heading": "Babur’s knife bargain and captivity",
						"text": "Airy’s warning about the depleted Orb leads Kir towards the observatory. Babur can open the blocked approach, but first demands the Chosen’s knife, now a sacred relic. While Kir pursues the relic, island orcs raid the hunters’ camp and carry Babur away. They hand him to their ogre neighbours after his dealings with them turn sour. Kir obtains the two keys to the pit, frees the craftsman and escorts him back alive. Babur renounces further trade with the orcs. His rescue also lets Bor put forward a new plan against the settlers.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_139",
							"briefing brief_140",
							"briefing brief_141",
							"briefing brief_142",
							"briefing brief_145a",
							"quest q24g",
							"script zone16"
						],
						"event": "babur_rescue",
						"order": 21
					},
					{
						"heading": "Bor’s attack on the new village",
						"text": "Bor’s final plan against the settlers uses the orcs’ old lightning pyramid. He supplies its key and asks Kir to activate it inside the new village, then escape its reach. The attack continues with orcs and Free Hunters finishing the surviving defenders. Kel calls the result a massacre and accuses Bor of using them for his dirty work. Bor answers that the settlers have retreated to the old village: he wanted to restore the balance disrupted by the Chosen. Kir understands that motive, while Kel’s objection remains part of the exchange. The restored order is Bor’s justification for the violence.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_143",
							"briefing brief_144",
							"quest q25g"
						],
						"event": "bor_pyramid",
						"order": 22
					},
					{
						"heading": "Babur’s rescue and the restored Orb",
						"text": "The stolen Orb stands on an island orc pedestal. Kir obtains the bridge gear from their gathering on Council Mountain to reach it. Once Babur is safe and has seen the damaged Orb, he provides the key to the observatory approach. The observatory book lets Estera prescribe washing the Orb in underground spring water. A landslide closes Kir’s former route to Tka-Rik, forcing him through the old tunnel. Before departure, Airy asks Kel to greet the Chosen if they meet him and says she still remembers him. Kel apologises for her earlier jealousy; both women recognise feelings Kir has yet to understand. The repaired Orb shows snow rather than Jigran. Tka-Rik interprets this as Ingos, agrees that Kel can go with Kir, and warns that greater freedom there does not prevent cruel government or make their search for powerful magic safe.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_138",
							"briefing brief_139",
							"briefing brief_140",
							"briefing brief_141",
							"briefing brief_142",
							"briefing brief_145b",
							"briefing brief_145c",
							"briefing brief_146",
							"briefing brief_148",
							"briefing brief_149",
							"briefing brief_151",
							"briefing brief_147",
							"briefing brief_150",
							"quest q22g",
							"briefing brief_145a"
						],
						"event": "orb_repair",
						"order": 23
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz11g",
				"id": "bz11g"
			},
			"map/bz12g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Estera’s cave remains a source of magical knowledge after Zak’s journey. Airy guards it in the witch’s absence. The visitors’ search for the Magic Orb becomes entangled with Estera’s flower, the Old Dragon and the two women’s disagreements.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz12g",
							"briefing brief_129",
							"briefing brief_133",
							"briefing brief_136",
							"briefing brief_137"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Airy, Estera and the Dragon’s flower",
						"text": "[[character/daughter]] guards the cave while Estera is visiting the [[character/skd|Old Dragon]]. Her quarrel with Kel over a necklace exposes Kel’s jealousy and Kir’s failure to notice it. Airy refuses his courtship and suggests looking at the person already beside him. At the dragon’s lair, Estera is the troublesome visitor rather than a prisoner: she refuses to leave without information about a flower. Kir obtains it and the witch goes home. [[character/witch]] then admits the Orb was stolen by orcs; Airy identifies the islands and warns that it lost its power after its earlier use.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_129",
							"briefing brief_130",
							"briefing brief_131",
							"briefing brief_132",
							"briefing brief_133",
							"briefing brief_134",
							"briefing brief_136",
							"briefing brief_137"
						],
						"event": "flower_dragon",
						"order": 20
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz12g",
				"id": "bz12g"
			},
			"map/bz13g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Dragon’s dwelling is another surviving landmark from Zak’s travels. The Old Dragon remembers the former Chosen but is currently more troubled by Estera’s demands about a flower. Kir and Kel must approach him through conversation and negotiation.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz13g",
							"briefing brief_132",
							"briefing brief_133"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Airy, Estera and the Dragon’s flower",
						"text": "[[character/daughter]] guards the cave while Estera is visiting the [[character/skd|Old Dragon]]. Her quarrel with Kel over a necklace exposes Kel’s jealousy and Kir’s failure to notice it. Airy refuses his courtship and suggests looking at the person already beside him. At the dragon’s lair, Estera is the troublesome visitor rather than a prisoner: she refuses to leave without information about a flower. Kir obtains it and the witch goes home. [[character/witch]] then admits the Orb was stolen by orcs; Airy identifies the islands and warns that it lost its power after its earlier use.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_129",
							"briefing brief_130",
							"briefing brief_131",
							"briefing brief_132",
							"briefing brief_133",
							"briefing brief_134",
							"briefing brief_136",
							"briefing brief_137"
						],
						"event": "flower_dragon",
						"order": 20
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz13g",
				"id": "bz13g"
			},
			"map/bz14g": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Tka-Rik’s underground home links the two campaigns. He explains that Zak and Lu-a-Jalla have left to travel, listens to Kir’s account of Jigran and interprets the Magic Orb. The resulting clue directs Kir and Kel to Ingos.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz14g",
							"briefing brief_118",
							"briefing brief_119",
							"briefing brief_149"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Tka-Rik and the unknown allod",
						"text": "Meeting [[character/rick]] gives Kir his first direct contact with a free Joon magician. Tka-Rik explains that At-Zako and Lu-a-Jalla are travelling together and that he himself remains bound to Gipath. He has never heard of Jigran. Kir tells him about Firez, his enslaved family and his promise to return. Learning that more Joons need help renews Tka-Rik’s purpose. Since he cannot locate the hidden allod unaided, he sends Kir to find the Magic Orb that At-Zako once used. Finding the magician therefore begins another investigation rather than immediately opening the road home.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_118",
							"briefing brief_119"
						],
						"event": "kir_tka",
						"order": 16
					},
					{
						"heading": "Babur’s rescue and the restored Orb",
						"text": "The stolen Orb stands on an island orc pedestal. Kir obtains the bridge gear from their gathering on Council Mountain to reach it. Once Babur is safe and has seen the damaged Orb, he provides the key to the observatory approach. The observatory book lets Estera prescribe washing the Orb in underground spring water. A landslide closes Kir’s former route to Tka-Rik, forcing him through the old tunnel. Before departure, Airy asks Kel to greet the Chosen if they meet him and says she still remembers him. Kel apologises for her earlier jealousy; both women recognise feelings Kir has yet to understand. The repaired Orb shows snow rather than Jigran. Tka-Rik interprets this as Ingos, agrees that Kel can go with Kir, and warns that greater freedom there does not prevent cruel government or make their search for powerful magic safe.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_138",
							"briefing brief_139",
							"briefing brief_140",
							"briefing brief_141",
							"briefing brief_142",
							"briefing brief_145b",
							"briefing brief_145c",
							"briefing brief_146",
							"briefing brief_148",
							"briefing brief_149",
							"briefing brief_151",
							"briefing brief_147",
							"briefing brief_150",
							"quest q22g",
							"briefing brief_145a"
						],
						"event": "orb_repair",
						"order": 23
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz14g",
				"id": "bz14g"
			},
			"map/bz21k": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The city of Ingos houses the governor, his court magician and the merchant Karansul. Their interests intersect with the murder inquiry: private relationships, a stolen nugget and the court’s own schemes all become relevant evidence.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz21k",
							"briefing brief_201",
							"briefing brief_224",
							"briefing brief_245a",
							"briefing brief_247",
							"briefing brief_250a"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Two magicians on Ingos",
						"text": "Kir and Kel seek magical help on Ingos. [[character/trader]] complains about [[character/kmage]], whose cheap runes undercut his trade, and identifies [[character/his]] as the governor’s court magician. His receives the travellers with suspicion and later asks them to retrieve materials from his laboratory. [[character/trapper]] offers another route: contact [[character/grey]] through the Sacred Flower and a vessel from his cache. Grey Leaf explains that the knight Brissen and his magician friend have left for the settlement near the mines. Kir’s first task here is finding an informed ally, before the murder that later dominates the investigation. The vessel Sver asks them to carry is for Woodsfolk balm that eases his own aching joints. He has used their necessary visit to obtain a personal favour.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_201",
							"briefing brief_204",
							"briefing brief_206",
							"briefing brief_207",
							"briefing brief_210",
							"briefing brief_211",
							"briefing brief_207a"
						],
						"event": "ingos_contacts",
						"order": 24
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Beastmasters and the empty caravan",
						"text": "At the Beastmasters’ settlement, [[character/vor]] accuses Kir and Kel of being spies. [[character/gerald]] agrees to test them. Their attempt to obtain a gold nugget from a caravan finds an empty chest, making their return dangerous. [[character/mthief]] reasons that genuine thieves would not come back empty-handed, and Geraldine accepts them. Advice from Trickster, also passed on by Sver if Kir consults him, about an ice elemental in the abandoned mines provides something to offer Yam-Akara. His work and the governor’s permit requirements then draw the travellers into further dealings with the court. The missing nugget will matter again when the apparent caravan failure becomes evidence of a separate theft.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_212",
							"briefing brief_215",
							"briefing brief_218",
							"briefing brief_221",
							"briefing brief_245a",
							"briefing brief_214",
							"briefing brief_217",
							"briefing brief_219"
						],
						"event": "beastmasters",
						"order": 26
					},
					{
						"heading": "Yam-Akara’s research and the governor’s gift",
						"text": "Yam-Akara introduces himself as a practising magician of the first rank and an honorary member of the Assembly of Magicians of Three Worlds. The elemental samples interest him, and the Joon artifact promises material for a scholarly report. He agrees to search while Kir renews his permit for the mines, where Wormface magic interferes with his measurements. His summons the travellers to Governor Valrasian whether or not his own laboratory errand is complete. The governor demands proper introductions, pays unwelcome attention to Kel and hints at a rare Blue Clan mage’s dart for his collection. Bringing the dart wins the permit, despite his complaints about Yam-Akara’s visits.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_219",
							"briefing brief_221",
							"briefing brief_222",
							"briefing brief_223",
							"briefing brief_224",
							"briefing brief_225",
							"quest q12k"
						],
						"event": "governor_gift",
						"order": 27
					},
					{
						"heading": "Brissen’s murder and the failed alibi",
						"text": "Green leaves around the settlement lead Kir to Grey Leaf, who brings the news. Brissen is murdered and Yam-Akara is accused of killing his old friend. Kir and Kel undertake the defence. The magician says he spent the day in the mines and arrived late for their meeting. [[character/guard]] confirms that he allowed the elderly visitor through with an expired permit and stamped it that day. Kir finds the lost pass, but [[character/huber]] rejects the water-damaged document because the signature and date can no longer be read. This evidence supports the search but does not free Yam-Akara; the travellers still have to identify the murderer.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_232",
							"briefing brief_233",
							"briefing brief_238",
							"briefing brief_239",
							"briefing brief_240",
							"briefing brief_228",
							"briefing brief_231"
						],
						"event": "brissen",
						"order": 30
					},
					{
						"heading": "The planted knife and His’s death",
						"text": "A knife found at the murder site appears to implicate His. The court magician admits it was stolen from his laboratory together with the governor’s nugget. He had sent out the empty caravan to hide that loss and hoped to blame raiders after an informant warned of the planned attack. His arranges a meeting to produce evidence, but is killed before he can clear himself. Meanwhile Geraldine and Karansul disclose their relationship and provide each other with an alibi. Their commercial hostility and private secrecy had made them suspicious without establishing guilt.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_243",
							"briefing brief_245",
							"briefing brief_245a",
							"briefing brief_246",
							"briefing brief_247"
						],
						"event": "his_murder",
						"order": 31
					},
					{
						"heading": "Crooked’s confession",
						"text": "The settlement’s missing communal money supplies the decisive motive. Kir and Kel catch Crooked at his cache. Brissen had discovered the theft, so Crooked killed him. He also stole His’s knife and the gold nugget, informed on the caravan raid and later planted the knife to shift suspicion. His called the same informant to clear his own name; Crooked mistook that invitation for a trap and killed him too. Woodsfolk observations help reconstruct the planted evidence. Before the governor, Kir explains the connected crimes and Crooked admits them. Yam-Akara is released, while the governor reserves Crooked’s punishment for trial. Kel draws the thief out by spreading word that they have found the hidden money. Afterwards the travellers return the recovered communal funds to Geraldine and Trickster. Geraldine wants personal revenge, but Crooked is already in the governor’s custody.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_237",
							"briefing brief_248",
							"briefing brief_249",
							"briefing brief_250",
							"briefing brief_250a",
							"briefing brief_251",
							"briefing brief_251a"
						],
						"event": "crooked",
						"order": 32
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz21k",
				"id": "bz21k"
			},
			"map/bz22k": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Beastmasters’ settlement brings together Geraldine, Crooked, Trickster and Yam-Akara. Suspicion after the empty caravan tests the newcomers’ reception. The truth about Brissen’s death ultimately exposes a crime within this community.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz22k",
							"briefing brief_212",
							"briefing brief_215",
							"briefing brief_250a"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Beastmasters and the empty caravan",
						"text": "At the Beastmasters’ settlement, [[character/vor]] accuses Kir and Kel of being spies. [[character/gerald]] agrees to test them. Their attempt to obtain a gold nugget from a caravan finds an empty chest, making their return dangerous. [[character/mthief]] reasons that genuine thieves would not come back empty-handed, and Geraldine accepts them. Advice from Trickster, also passed on by Sver if Kir consults him, about an ice elemental in the abandoned mines provides something to offer Yam-Akara. His work and the governor’s permit requirements then draw the travellers into further dealings with the court. The missing nugget will matter again when the apparent caravan failure becomes evidence of a separate theft.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_212",
							"briefing brief_215",
							"briefing brief_218",
							"briefing brief_221",
							"briefing brief_245a",
							"briefing brief_214",
							"briefing brief_217",
							"briefing brief_219"
						],
						"event": "beastmasters",
						"order": 26
					},
					{
						"heading": "Geraldine’s secret meetings",
						"text": "Trickster notices that Geraldine repeatedly leaves the settlement in secret. As its leader, he argues, she should not keep matters from her people. He asks Kir and Kel to follow her. They see a meeting with a man, but magical traps prevent them from approaching unnoticed and frighten the pair away. Their first report therefore establishes a secret meeting, not the man’s identity. Suspicion later falls on both His and Karansul; the eventual explanation is Geraldine’s relationship with the merchant, whom she plans to marry.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_228",
							"briefing brief_230",
							"briefing brief_247"
						],
						"event": "geraldine_secret",
						"order": 28
					},
					{
						"heading": "The planted knife and His’s death",
						"text": "A knife found at the murder site appears to implicate His. The court magician admits it was stolen from his laboratory together with the governor’s nugget. He had sent out the empty caravan to hide that loss and hoped to blame raiders after an informant warned of the planned attack. His arranges a meeting to produce evidence, but is killed before he can clear himself. Meanwhile Geraldine and Karansul disclose their relationship and provide each other with an alibi. Their commercial hostility and private secrecy had made them suspicious without establishing guilt.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_243",
							"briefing brief_245",
							"briefing brief_245a",
							"briefing brief_246",
							"briefing brief_247"
						],
						"event": "his_murder",
						"order": 31
					},
					{
						"heading": "Crooked’s confession",
						"text": "The settlement’s missing communal money supplies the decisive motive. Kir and Kel catch Crooked at his cache. Brissen had discovered the theft, so Crooked killed him. He also stole His’s knife and the gold nugget, informed on the caravan raid and later planted the knife to shift suspicion. His called the same informant to clear his own name; Crooked mistook that invitation for a trap and killed him too. Woodsfolk observations help reconstruct the planted evidence. Before the governor, Kir explains the connected crimes and Crooked admits them. Yam-Akara is released, while the governor reserves Crooked’s punishment for trial. Kel draws the thief out by spreading word that they have found the hidden money. Afterwards the travellers return the recovered communal funds to Geraldine and Trickster. Geraldine wants personal revenge, but Crooked is already in the governor’s custody.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_237",
							"briefing brief_248",
							"briefing brief_249",
							"briefing brief_250",
							"briefing brief_250a",
							"briefing brief_251",
							"briefing brief_251a"
						],
						"event": "crooked",
						"order": 32
					},
					{
						"heading": "The hidden portal to Jigran",
						"text": "During his confinement, Yam-Akara gains access to the governor’s library. Old exploration reports reveal that the powerful “artifact” is a forgotten passage to another land. Trickster offers a key to the gate, then sends Kir on a fruitless ore search so that he can work without interference. With the key ready, Yam-Akara warns that he cannot sense the destination: the portal may be defective or concealed by a powerful absorbing spell. Kir recognises the same absence Tka-Rik described and concludes that it leads to Jigran. He decides to go directly, trusting the strength gained during his journey. Kel chooses to accompany him again.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_251",
							"briefing brief_252",
							"briefing brief_253",
							"briefing brief_254"
						],
						"event": "old_portal",
						"order": 33
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz22k",
				"id": "bz22k"
			},
			"map/bz23k": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "The Green Clan’s new home preserves the Woodsfolk presence on Ingos. Grey Leaf supplies memories of Brissen and clues for the investigation, while elder Khabburu’s requests express a concern for freedom extending even to captive animals.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz23k",
							"briefing brief_207",
							"briefing fq15"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Two magicians on Ingos",
						"text": "Kir and Kel seek magical help on Ingos. [[character/trader]] complains about [[character/kmage]], whose cheap runes undercut his trade, and identifies [[character/his]] as the governor’s court magician. His receives the travellers with suspicion and later asks them to retrieve materials from his laboratory. [[character/trapper]] offers another route: contact [[character/grey]] through the Sacred Flower and a vessel from his cache. Grey Leaf explains that the knight Brissen and his magician friend have left for the settlement near the mines. Kir’s first task here is finding an informed ally, before the murder that later dominates the investigation. The vessel Sver asks them to carry is for Woodsfolk balm that eases his own aching joints. He has used their necessary visit to obtain a personal favour.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_201",
							"briefing brief_204",
							"briefing brief_206",
							"briefing brief_207",
							"briefing brief_210",
							"briefing brief_211",
							"briefing brief_207a"
						],
						"event": "ingos_contacts",
						"order": 24
					},
					{
						"heading": "Grey Leaf and the poisoned river",
						"text": "Grey Leaf reports that animals are dying after drinking from the northern river. His healers cannot save them while Karansul’s factory keeps discharging poison into the water. Kel recognises that treating the animals alone cannot solve the problem. Karansul proudly confirms his ownership and describes a blasting powder used in his mines. Kir can obtain the powder and destroy the polluting works. Grey Leaf thanks him for ending the contamination. This request concerns the Woodsfolk’s protection of the forest, separately from the search for the Joon artifact.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_208",
							"briefing brief_208b",
							"briefing brief_209",
							"quest q7k"
						],
						"event": "poisoned_river",
						"order": 25
					},
					{
						"heading": "Crooked’s confession",
						"text": "The settlement’s missing communal money supplies the decisive motive. Kir and Kel catch Crooked at his cache. Brissen had discovered the theft, so Crooked killed him. He also stole His’s knife and the gold nugget, informed on the caravan raid and later planted the knife to shift suspicion. His called the same informant to clear his own name; Crooked mistook that invitation for a trap and killed him too. Woodsfolk observations help reconstruct the planted evidence. Before the governor, Kir explains the connected crimes and Crooked admits them. Yam-Akara is released, while the governor reserves Crooked’s punishment for trial. Kel draws the thief out by spreading word that they have found the hidden money. Afterwards the travellers return the recovered communal funds to Geraldine and Trickster. Geraldine wants personal revenge, but Crooked is already in the governor’s custody.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_237",
							"briefing brief_248",
							"briefing brief_249",
							"briefing brief_250",
							"briefing brief_250a",
							"briefing brief_251",
							"briefing brief_251a"
						],
						"event": "crooked",
						"order": 32
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz23k",
				"id": "bz23k"
			},
			"map/bz31j": {
				"kind": "map",
				"sections": [
					{
						"heading": "Setting",
						"text": "Dar-Leanos is the Canian stronghold where Kir meets Baron Virril, his son Dartas and Master Stark. Losses in the war and recurring undead attacks shape its needs. The city becomes the base for the struggle to reach Firez and free Jigran.",
						"sources": [
							"zone bz31j",
							"briefing brief_302",
							"briefing brief_303",
							"briefing brief_304"
						]
					},
					{
						"heading": "Return to Jigran and Dar-Leanos",
						"text": "The old portal brings the travellers to the Canian side of Jigran, where [[character/baron]] questions their arrival amid attacks by the undead. The baron has lost Dar-Ulgorn to Firez’s forces but refuses Kir’s suggestion to seek outside help, citing his family’s pride. Kir and Kel assist the endangered settlements. They meet the baron’s son [[character/bson]] and the overworked [[character/stark]]. Stark provides a password for [[character/doctor]], a seller of sweets who secretly gathers intelligence for the baron. These local alliances make the campaign against Firez possible.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_300",
							"briefing brief_301",
							"briefing brief_302",
							"briefing brief_303",
							"briefing brief_304",
							"briefing brief_305",
							"briefing brief_306"
						],
						"event": "dar_leanos",
						"order": 34
					},
					{
						"heading": "Jaminin’s experiment and the undead",
						"text": "The trail of the local undead leads to [[character/nekro]] on Death Island. Brought before the baron, he bargains for his life with the promise of useful knowledge. Kir and Kel obtain a potion from a tomb, but need to know how to apply it across the affected area. Jaminin first entertains a human sacrifice; Kel rejects it, and he supplies a less destructive method involving cemetery soil and an incantation. The potion puts the dead to rest. His later exchanges preserve the distinction between theoretical learning and practical competence: he possesses information, but his own experiment caused the disaster. Their success also changes the baron’s view of Kel. He admits that her achievements have repeatedly disproved his claim that war is no business for women.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_307",
							"briefing brief_311",
							"briefing brief_311a",
							"briefing brief_312",
							"briefing brief_313",
							"briefing brief_315",
							"briefing brief_316"
						],
						"event": "jaminin",
						"order": 36
					},
					{
						"heading": "Dartas, Kel and the duel",
						"text": "Dartas treats Kel as someone he is entitled to possess, insults her attachment to Kir and refuses to accept rejection. When Kir confronts him, Dartas asks what he wants in exchange for her and then calls him a coward. They duel on Death Island. Defeated and spared, Dartas admits despair over Dar-Ulgorn’s loss and his family’s future. Kir answers with his own enslavement, escape and determination to fight, winning Dartas’s respect. Kel initially believes the duel means that Kir has recognised her feelings; he disappoints her by calling it a defence of his own honour. The baron considers punishment, then accepts that Dartas needed the lesson; Kir asks him not to punish his son further.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_314",
							"briefing brief_317",
							"briefing brief_318",
							"briefing brief_324",
							"briefing brief_317a",
							"briefing brief_323"
						],
						"event": "dartas_duel",
						"order": 37
					},
					{
						"heading": "Weapons, Dartas and preparations for war",
						"text": "Kir proposes a special weapon alloy against the undead, drawing on a book he read. Stark sends him to an abandoned forge rather than allow a youngster to work in his own smithy. When Kir returns with the alloy, Kel recognises the source of the craftsman’s resistance: a hereditary master is ashamed of learning something his father never taught him. Kir presents the discovery as knowledge Stark can pass to his own son. The smith agrees to try it, and the baron welcomes the advantage for his soldiers. The improved weapons strengthen his preparations for an offensive.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_318",
							"briefing brief_319",
							"briefing brief_320",
							"briefing brief_321",
							"briefing brief_324",
							"briefing brief_336",
							"briefing brief_337"
						],
						"event": "preparing_war",
						"order": 38
					},
					{
						"heading": "Dar-Ulgorn and the river crossing",
						"text": "Rad leaves his troop report in a cemetery cache; collecting herbs gives him an innocent reason to visit it. Kir and Kel deliver the report, gather poisonous mushrooms and a pumpkin for Rad’s stew, then take the prepared meal to the enemy mess hall. The pumpkin prolongs the mushrooms’ laxative effect, keeping defenders away from their posts during the assault. Uncle Rad’s intelligence and preparations distract the defenders of Dar-Ulgorn so the baron’s forces can retake the fortress. The victory restores the family’s former boundary, but Virril will not advance blindly into Khadaganian territory. Kir and Kel steal a detailed map from the enemy headquarters and deliver Rad’s report. The resulting offensive captures the river crossing and brings Firez within reach. Kel worries that the end of Kir’s family quest will also end their life together, while the baron imagines a prosperous Jigran under his descendants. The victory means different things to the people who share it.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_326",
							"briefing brief_329",
							"briefing brief_330",
							"briefing brief_331",
							"briefing brief_332",
							"briefing brief_333",
							"briefing brief_334",
							"briefing brief_338",
							"briefing brief_339",
							"briefing brief_322",
							"briefing brief_325",
							"briefing brief_327",
							"quest q10j",
							"quest q11j"
						],
						"event": "offensive",
						"order": 39
					},
					{
						"heading": "The freed Joons and Kir’s family",
						"text": "Kel and Rad discover that Firez is moving the Joon prisoners from the mountain prison into his fortress. Kel plans an attack on the transfer, avoiding a direct prison assault. Among those freed are [[character/deva]], whom Kir admired as a child, and the elder [[character/junofil]]. Tai-Ilara welcomes his changed appearance and asks him to retrieve belongings left behind. Tui-Len brings harder news: Kir’s parents are dead. The elder fears that killing Firez will expose the survivors to the Curse, but Kir tells him that Tka-Rik and At-Zako have already destroyed it. Tui-Len also opens the sealed Joon cache whose amulet Kir has recovered.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_339",
							"briefing brief_340",
							"briefing brief_341",
							"briefing brief_335",
							"briefing brief_336",
							"briefing brief_337"
						],
						"event": "joons_freed",
						"order": 40
					},
					{
						"heading": "Tai-Ilara’s request and Kel’s death",
						"text": "Seeing Kir reunited with Tai-Ilara, Kel asks for the way back to Suslanger. Kir begs her to stay and mentions one more expedition for Tai-Ilara’s belongings. Kel agrees because she still fears for him, calling it their last adventure together. She dies during that expedition. On Kir’s return, Tai-Ilara is concerned with her comb and mirror and dismisses his grief. Her reaction breaks his idealised childhood attachment. He recognises Kel as the person who was closest and most loyal to him, and realises that he failed to see her love while she was beside him.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_342",
							"briefing brief_345",
							"briefing fq20"
						],
						"event": "kel_loss",
						"order": 41
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Rod of Life: resurrecting Kel",
						"text": "Kel can be resurrected in the original Lost in Astral campaign. Her scripted death still occurs, but if Kir has all three parts of the Rod of Life when he finishes the conversation with [[character/deva|Tai-Ilara]] about her retrieved belongings, [[character/junofil|Tui-Len]] offers to bring Kel back. Completing his conversation consumes the fragments and returns Kel alive to the playable party before the final battle, with her saved skills, experience and equipment. Speak to her afterward for their reunion: Kir finally declares his love and asks forgiveness for his blindness. Kel answers that his love matters more than everything else.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_345",
							"briefing fq20",
							"briefing fq21",
							"script cz3j",
							"script bz31j"
						],
						"event": "rod_of_life",
						"order": 42
					},
					{
						"heading": "The Joon cache and allies for the assault",
						"text": "The sealed cache supplies Kir with Joon armour, but Tui-Len warns him against relying on hatred alone. He gives him an amulet that can turn dangerous creatures into allies. Rad identifies suitable hunting grounds for a cyclops and a dragon. Their recruitment changes the military balance and answers the baron’s objection that his soldiers cannot face Firez’s necromancy in a direct assault. Virril finally orders the attack. Before the confrontation, Rad speaks of the quiet home and family he hopes to have when his secret work is finished. The cache is found in the valley Rad hopes to settle after the war. Dartas’s account of a nearby village destroyed by harpies points Kir to the rectangular amulet needed at its sealed entrance; Tui-Len supplies the remaining magical help. During the assault, Firez sends the city’s population, transformed into undead, against the Canian force. Kir must break through this army to reach the palace.",
						"sources": [
							"briefing brief_343",
							"briefing brief_344",
							"briefing brief_347",
							"briefing brief_348",
							"briefing brief_330",
							"briefing brief_336",
							"briefing brief_341",
							"quest q21j"
						],
						"event": "allies",
						"order": 43
					}
				],
				"name_key": "zone bz31j",
				"id": "bz31j"
			},
			"map/bz32j": {
